text: ['({T}: Add {B}.)', 'All Rats have fear.\\n{T}, Sacrifice a Rat: Create X 1/1 black Rat creature tokens, where X is the number of Rats you control.', "All creatures gain fear until end of turn. (They can't be blocked except by artifact creatures and/or black creatures.)\\nCycling {1}{B} ({1}{B}, Discard this card: Draw a card.)\\nWhen you cycle Dirge of Dread, you may have target creature gain fear until end of turn.", "At the beginning of your upkeep, target opponent may exile a card from their graveyard. If that player doesn't, you may draw a card.", 'Black creatures get +2/+0 until end of turn.', 'Choose one —\\n• Destroy target creature.\\n• Return target creature card from your graveyard to the battlefield.\\nEntwine—Sacrifice three lands. (Choose both if you pay the entwine cost.)', "Destroy target nonblack creature. It can't be regenerated. You gain life equal to its toughness.", 'Draw four cards. You lose half your life, rounded up.', 'Exile X target cards from a single graveyard.', "Fear (This creature can't be blocked except by artifact creatures and/or black creatures.)\\nNezumi Cutthroat can't block.", "Ninjutsu {B} ({B}, Return an unblocked attacker you control to hand: Put this card onto the battlefield from your hand tapped and attacking.)\\nWhenever Skullsnatcher deals combat damage to a player, exile up to two target cards from that player's graveyard.", "Rat offering (You may cast this card any time you could cast an instant by sacrificing a Rat and paying the difference in mana costs between this and the sacrificed Rat. Mana cost includes color.)\\nWhenever a permanent is put into an opponent's graveyard, that player loses 1 life.", 'Search your library for a card, put that card into your hand, then shuffle.', "Swarm of Rats's power is equal to the number of Rats you control.", 'Target creature gets -2/-2 until end of turn.\\nFlashback—{1}{B}, Pay 3 life. (You may cast this card from your graveyard for its flashback cost. Then exile it.)', "Target player exiles all cards from their hand face down. At the beginning of the end step of that player's next turn, that player returns those cards to their hand.", 'When Chittering Rats enters the battlefield, target opponent puts a card from their hand on top of their library.', 'Whenever Carrion Rats attacks or blocks, any player may exile a card from their graveyard. If a player does, Carrion Rats assigns no combat damage this turn.', 'Whenever a creature you control deals combat damage to a player, that player discards a card.', "{1}{B}: Exile target card from an opponent's graveyard. If no cards are in that graveyard, flip Nezumi Graverobber.", '{4}{B}: Put target creature card from a graveyard onto the battlefield under your control.', "{B}, Discard a card: Regenerate Dirty Wererat.\\nThreshold — As long as seven or more cards are in your graveyard, Dirty Wererat gets +2/+2 and can't block.", '{B}, Sacrifice a creature: Target player discards a card. Activate only as a sorcery.', '{X}: Crypt Rats deals X damage to each creature and each player. Spend only black mana on X.']
flavor: ['"East and West meet and angels cry.\\nUnholy power darkens the sky."\\n—"The Ballad of the Paladins"', '"Just what I need—more competition."\\n—Cabal grave robber', '"Rats, rats, rats! Hundreds, thousands, millions of them, and every one a life."\\n—Bram Stoker, *Dracula*', '"That does it—I quit!"\\n—Cabal grave robber', '"The mortal world is a grove of sweet fruit, and I am ready to harvest it."\\n—Fallen angel', '"These nezumi, they disgust me. The things they will do for money no other thinking creature would consider. This, of course, makes them useful beyond words."\\n—Meloku the Clouded Mirror', 'Beware anything born among the dead.\\n—Tolarian saving', 'Bottom feeders sometimes rise to the top.', 'Four generations ago, old Split-Tail stole a bundle of scrolls from the Minamo School. Soon after, the first nezumi shamans were born.', 'Marrow-Gnawer united three nezumi gangs when he slew their leaders in a single night. Now they call him their first lord.', 'Opportunity makes the thief.\\n—Roman proverb', "The Eastern Paladin doesn't attack at night for the tactical advantage. He does it for the sheer joy of fighting beneath the moon.", 'The best ideas often come from the worst minds.']
